 highest play-action rate in the league.","offense":"The Bears offense has been a little above average by EPA per play so far. Their run and pass mix has stayed close to what game situations call for. Their biggest outliers in how they line up are the highest play-action rate in the league. Caleb Williams has run the passing game, with Luther Burden III leading the team in targets.","defense":"The Bears defense has been well above average by EPA allowed per play so far. Its strongest area has been sack rate, where it ranks 3rd, and its weakest has been explosive play rate allowed, where it ranks 22nd. Montez Sweat has been as productive as expected, but the bigger surprise is Kyler Gordon, who has 4 sacks despite playing only 56% of the snaps. Their scheme has been fairly conventional, and the closest thing to a signature is a number of defenders in the box that ranks 4th highest in the league.","special":"The Bears have been one of the most aggressive teams on fourth down, going for it on 50% of their short-yardage chances so far. Their kickers have made 79% of field goals, which is a little below average. On the roster side, they put an unusually large share of their salary cap into tight ends, about 8% of the money on the books.","players":"Caleb Williams has played nearly every snap at quarterback and thrown for 1,546 yards. Luther Burden III has been the clear top target with 22% of the looks and 644 receiving yards. D'Andre Swift has handled most of the work in the backfield with 60% of the carries. On defense, Austin Booker leads the team with 5 sacks.","coaching":"Ben Johnson is calling the offense as head coach and Dennis Allen is calling the defense. Their offense looks most like the 2025 Vikings under Kevin O'Connell and the 2022 Lions under Ben Johnson. Their defense looks most like the 2023 Cowboys under Dan Quinn and the 2024 Jets under Jeff Ulbrich.","trends":"The offense went from 8th in EPA per play in 2025 to 13th so far this season, a modest decline. The defense moved from 19th in EPA allowed per play in 2025 to 10th so far this season, a big step forward. The biggest change in approach was two point attempt rate, which went from 12% to 0%.","notes":"The 2026 pages are built from data that nflverse updates every week, so they will keep changing until the season ends. Personnel, formations, coverage and pressure numbers are missing for now because that data is only released after the season."}}